freeglut — OSMesa (headless) backend and the standalone "glutshapes" library
============================================================================

This file documents two related, optional build configurations:

  1. FREEGLUT_OSMESA       — a headless freeglut backend that renders with no
                             window system at all (off-screen, via OSMesa).
  2. FREEGLUT_BUILD_GLUTSHAPES — a standalone library of just freeglut's
                             geometry + font drawing, with no windowing, for
                             environments that already emulate GLUT windowing.

They are independent CMake options and can be built separately or together.


1. OSMesa backend (FREEGLUT_OSMESA)
-----------------------------------

Normally freeglut creates the GL context through the platform's native window
system (GLX/X11, WGL/Windows, NSOpenGL/Cocoa, EGL/GLES). The OSMesa backend
instead uses OSMesa ("Off-Screen Mesa"), which renders into a plain CPU memory
buffer with NO X11/GLX/Cocoa/EGL and no display server. The result is a
software (llvmpipe/swrast) **compatibility** GL context, so fixed-function
rendering, freeglut's glutSolid*/glutWire* shapes, and glRenderMode(GL_FEEDBACK)
all work — which makes it ideal for headless CI (e.g. validating geometry or
feedback output with no display).

It is OS-agnostic (Linux, macOS, Windows) and is mutually exclusive with the
FREEGLUT_GLES, FREEGLUT_WAYLAND and FREEGLUT_COCOA options (CMake errors out if
they are combined).

Dependencies:
  - Linux:   apt-get install libosmesa6-dev   (pulls llvmpipe)
  - macOS:   brew install mesa                 (provides libOSMesa + GL/osmesa.h)
  - Windows: a Mesa build providing osmesa.{h,lib/dll}

Build:
    cmake -DFREEGLUT_OSMESA=ON -DFREEGLUT_GLES=OFF -B build-osmesa .
    cmake --build build-osmesa

This produces the library named `glut_osmesa` (so it coexists with a normal
freeglut build) plus a self-test, `osmesa_selftest`, which renders with no
display and checks read-back and GL_FEEDBACK capture:

    ./build-osmesa/bin/osmesa_selftest

Notes / limitations:
  - Single buffer only. GLUT_DOUBLE is accepted but downgraded to single-buffer
    (glutSwapBuffers is a no-op; the draw buffer is pinned to GL_FRONT).
  - The context is always a compatibility context; glutInitContextProfile with a
    core profile is ignored (a compatibility context is the whole point).
  - GLUT_ACCUM, GLUT_MULTISAMPLE and GLUT_SRGB are best-effort/ignored on swrast.
  - No input devices, cursor, game mode, menus or window resizing
    (glutReshapeWindow does not reallocate the off-screen buffer).
  - The general progs/ demos (which use GLU and assume a window) are not built
    under FREEGLUT_OSMESA.
  - Read pixels back with OSMesaGetColorBuffer(); with the Gallium OSMesa driver
    the finished image is not guaranteed to be in your malloc'd pointer until a
    flush. OSMesa's default pixel origin is bottom-up (OSMESA_Y_UP=1).


2. Standalone "glutshapes" library (FREEGLUT_BUILD_GLUTSHAPES)
-------------------------------------------------------------

This builds *only* freeglut's geometry and font drawing — glutSolidTeapot,
glutWireSphere, the rest of the solids, and glutStrokeString/glutBitmapString —
into a self-contained static library (`glutshapes`) with NONE of freeglut's
windowing, event loop, or context code. It is meant for environments that
already provide the GLUT windowing API and a GL context themselves (notably
Emscripten, optionally with gl4es for legacy GL on top of WebGL) and just want
freeglut's shapes and fonts.

    cmake -DFREEGLUT_BUILD_GLUTSHAPES=ON -B build .
    cmake --build build --target glutshapes

The shape and font entry points keep their normal glut* names (that is the
product you link against); the handful of freeglut-internal support symbols the
geometry needs are renamed to private fgshapes_* names so the library is
self-contained and does not collide with — or accidentally bind to — a host
that already implements the GLUT/GL support API.

A consumer includes <GL/freeglut.h> for the shape/font prototypes and, for the
few standalone hooks, <GL/glutshapes.h>:

    void glutShapesSetProcAddressFunc(void *(*resolver)(const char *name));
    void glutShapesSetGLVersion(int major);
    void glutShapesInit(void);

Draw paths and the GLES/WebGL caveat:
  - By default the GL 1.1 fixed-function path is used (client vertex arrays for
    the solids; glBitmap / immediate-mode glBegin for the fonts). This works on
    desktop GL, on the OSMesa backend above, and on **gl4es** (which emulates
    these legacy calls on top of GLES) — gl4es is therefore the natural
    companion for using glutshapes in the browser.
  - On a raw GLES2/WebGL context (no gl4es) only the GL 2.0 vertex-attrib path
    is valid: build with -DFREEGLUT_GLES, call glutShapesSetGLVersion(2) (or
    glutShapesInit after installing a resolver), bind a shader, and pass the
    attribute locations via glutSetVertexAttribCoord3/Normal/TexCoord2. The
    fixed-function fonts require gl4es in that case.

When OSMesa is also enabled, a functional test `glutshapes_selftest` is built
that drives glutshapes with a raw OSMesa context and no freeglut windowing
linked, confirming the solids and stroke fonts render standalone.
